title: Simplified grammar notation
tags: [[Simplified query language]]
kind: reference
summary: The fixed lexer, the grammar notation (ordered choice, repetitions, captures) and the template language that writes the DSL.

!! The lexer (fixed)

The input is cut into tokens by a tokenizer you cannot configure; whitespace between
tokens is dropped.

|!Token |!Matches |
|`UUID` |exactly 32 hex characters, maximal on both sides — tried first, since a uuid may start like a number or a word |
|`WORD` |`[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` |
|`NUMBER` |an integer or float |
|`STRING` |a double-quoted literal, with escapes |
|`SYMBOL` |a maximal run of punctuation (`:`, `~`, `>=`, `..`, `+`, `#=`…) |
|`(` `)` |each its own token |

So `genre=jazz` and `genre = jazz` lex alike, as do `100MB` and `100 MB`; maximal munch
gives word boundaries for free (a literal `"tag"` never matches `tags`). A hex run that
is not exactly 32 long is a `NUMBER` or a `WORD` — the lexer reports nothing, the
grammar decides.

!! The grammar

A list of named productions; the start production is `query`.

|!Form |!Means |
|`name = …` |a production |
|`a b` |sequence |
|`a*`, `a+`, `a?` |zero or more, one or more, optional |
|`a ** s`, `a ++ s` |zero or more, one or more, separated by `s` |
|`( … )` |grouping |
|`"lit"` |one token whose text is `lit` |
|`WORD` `NUMBER` `STRING` `UUID` |one token of that class |
|`label:item` |binds the match (a list, for a repetition) |
|`item => template` |the production's output |
|`# …` |a comment |

Alternatives are separated by a vertical bar, `a | b`: an ''ordered choice'' — the
first alternative that succeeds wins, with no backtracking afterwards. So of two
alternatives starting alike, the longer must come first (`"!?"` before `"?"`).

A production without `=>` returns the source text it matched (a `STRING` keeps its
quotes, a `WORD` stays bare).

!! Templates

Right of `=>`, a template is literal text with holes: `$name` interpolates a capture's
text verbatim, `{ expr }` evaluates an expression. Adjacent text and captures simply
concatenate. Everything is text, and nothing converts implicitly — `"007"` stays
`"007"` — so numbers are asked for:

|!In `{ }` |!Does |
|`$name` |the capture's text |
|`+ - * / %` |arithmetic on numbers (integer unless an operand is a float) |
|`num(x)` |text to number, an error if it is not one |
|`str(x)` |`x` as a DSL string literal — idempotent: a bare word is quoted, a quoted string left alone |
|`join(sep, list)` |a repetition's captures joined by `sep` |
|`now()` |the current time, Unix milliseconds — so a rule using it expands differently from one moment to the next |

The set is deliberately small; a new function is an engine addition that breaks no
grammar.
